 "_status": "On the Implementation Funding tab you can now drag a priority into a different position — Priority 3 into the Priority 1 slot, say — and everything comes with it: its wording, its metric, its share, its factor and its list of recommended strategies. Nothing about the saved figures changes; the order is kept separately, so the money each priority carries is identical before and after, and there is a Reset button. \"Price factor\" is now \"Funding factor\", and the numbers still recalculate as you type. On the request to copy Year 1 into Year 2 whenever Front-load is picked: that would have overwritten Year 2 with no way back, as a side effect of a switch about payment timing — so instead there is a \"Year 2 mirrors Year 1\" tick box that keeps them in step without destroying anything, plus a separate Copy button that asks first. Both are off until you turn them on. One knock-on was worth catching: the My College page pairs each priority's money with its advice by POSITION, so reordering would have attached the wrong steps to the wrong pot — that now pairs by identity. Everything from the Session 172 queue below is still open and unchanged.",
 "items": [
  {
   "id": "s173-funding-reorder-try",
   "for": "Sam",
   "tab": "implementation-funding",
   "text": "Have a go at the reorder in a browser. Each priority card has a Drag handle and a Position box — drag Priority 3 onto the first slot, or just set its Position to 1. Everything travels with the card and the dollar figures do not move; a Reset order button appears once the order differs from what is saved. Then set the new shares and funding factors — they recalculate as you go, and the balance box will warn you if the three shares stop adding to 100%. Two things to decide while you are in there: whether the order is right, and whether to tick \"Year 2 mirrors Year 1\". Your Scenario 1 already holds identical years, so ticking it costs nothing and makes them impossible to drift apart — but it ships off, so it is your call."
  },
  {
   "id": "s173-ci-one-file",
   "for": "Fable",
   "tab": "implementation-funding",
   "text": "Finish PR #1268. The security check passed; the test check reported one file failing out of 231. Everything that touches the changed code was re-run and is green, which leaves tests/cpl_funding.test.js as the only unchecked file — it takes about two minutes on the build server and over twenty-five in our sandbox, so it is slow rather than stuck. main was green earlier today, so this is ours. Reproduce it, fix it, push and merge."
  },
  {
   "id": "s172-guidance-surface",
   "for": "Sam",
   "tab": "map-data-quality",
   "text": "The list you asked for is built and nobody can see it. For each of the 225 military exhibits behind the 'no course named' records, we now hold what the training actually was — MOS-31B-002 is Military Police, MOS-68W-001 is Health Care Specialist, MOS-42A-001 is Human Resources Specialist — plus, where colleges have already articulated that exhibit, which course they used. Two decisions are yours. First, where it goes: I'd suggest a lookup panel behind the Credit by Exam row on the MAP Data Quality tab rather than another queue, because 225 exhibits is reference material, not 225 things to do. Second, whether the middle tier is worth showing at all — see the next item."
  },
  {
   "id": "s172-tier2-question",
   "for": "Sam",
   "tab": "map-data-quality",
   "text": "The 'where might this align' tag is weaker than it first looked, and you should know why before deciding what to show. Only 3 of 225 exhibits have a course that two or more colleges chose AND that isn't a catch-all: Military Police to Introduction to the Administration of Justice, and Human Resources Specialist to HR Management and Intro to Computer Information Systems. The third is a placeholder. My first attempt looked far better — 14 exhibits — until I noticed 11 of them pointed at the same course, Elements of Supervision, which three colleges apply to any military service at all: it appears against 33 different exhibits. So the titles are the real deliverable and the peer-course tag is mostly noise. Question for you: is the middle tier (47 exhibits where somebody named something, but nothing solid) useful to a counsellor, or just clutter?"
  },
  {
